WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.613 --> 00:00:06.194
The nineteenth century was the time of great
inventors and great inventions.

00:00:06.194 --> 00:00:10.184
Along the way, Thomas Edison became one of
the best-known and most admired inventors

00:00:10.184 --> 00:00:11.564
in history.

00:00:11.564 --> 00:00:14.714
Nikola Tesla is arguably less well-known.

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:18.954
Only recently has he attracted renewed attention
thanks in part to the electric car company

00:00:18.954 --> 00:00:20.614
that bears his name.

00:00:20.614 --> 00:00:27.143
In 1884, when Tesla was 28 years old, he sailed
for New York with only four cents in his pocket.

00:00:27.143 --> 00:00:32.234
His money and some of his luggage were stolen
on the way over.

00:00:32.234 --> 00:00:35.474
Like other immigrants, Tesla came to America
for work.

00:00:35.474 --> 00:00:39.064
He had been hired as an engineer at Edison
Machine Works in Manhattan.

00:00:39.064 --> 00:00:43.334
Tesla admired Edison, recalling: "One of the
great events in my life was my first meeting

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:44.634
with Edison.

00:00:44.634 --> 00:00:49.294
This wonderful man, who had received no scientific
training, yet had accomplished so much, filled

00:00:49.294 --> 00:00:51.024
me with amazement.”

00:00:51.024 --> 00:00:55.144
Edison was also impressed with the young engineer,
remarking to an assistant: “...this is a

00:00:55.144 --> 00:00:56.334
damn good man!”

00:00:56.334 --> 00:01:00.754
It didn’t take long, however, for things
to turn sour as the two men disagreed over

00:01:00.754 --> 00:01:04.024
how electricity should be delivered to the
masses.

00:01:04.024 --> 00:01:09.274
In 1879, Edison created the first practical
incandescent lightbulb and then built a system

00:01:09.274 --> 00:01:12.794
to carry electricity so people could use his
new invention.

00:01:12.794 --> 00:01:17.503
He opened up his first power plant in New
York in 1882 supported by his Direct Current

00:01:17.503 --> 00:01:20.434
electrical system known as DC.

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:24.734
A network of underground cables would carry
electricity from a generator to homes and

00:01:24.734 --> 00:01:25.734
businesses.

00:01:25.734 --> 00:01:27.933
But his DC generators kept failing.

00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:31.073
So he hired the young Serbian engineer to
improve them.

00:01:31.073 --> 00:01:35.054
Tesla saw the weaknesses of direct current
which travels through a power line in one

00:01:35.054 --> 00:01:37.103
direction at a constant voltage.

00:01:37.103 --> 00:01:42.003
But there’s a critical flaw: it can only
go up to a mile before losing power.

00:01:42.003 --> 00:01:45.984
Tesla felt he had come up with a better solution:
alternating current.

00:01:45.984 --> 00:01:50.054
AC periodically changes direction and can
also vary its voltage.

00:01:50.054 --> 00:01:55.024
The crucial advantage is that it can travel
hundreds of miles without losing power.

00:01:55.024 --> 00:01:59.634
Yet when Tesla tried to convince Edison of
the advantages of AC, his boss wasn’t having

00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:00.634
any of it.

00:02:00.634 --> 00:02:04.594
Edison didn’t want to lose the royalties
he earned from his DC patents.

00:02:04.594 --> 00:02:09.584
Tesla claimed Edison promised him $50,000
if he could improve on his DC generators and

00:02:09.584 --> 00:02:14.194
when he did, Edison refused to pay up and
is said to have responded: “Tesla, you don’t

00:02:14.194 --> 00:02:16.254
understand our American humor”.

00:02:16.254 --> 00:02:19.924
So Tesla quit after just a few months on the
job, writing in his diary:

00:02:19.924 --> 00:02:22.674
“Good by to the Edison Machine Works”.

00:02:22.674 --> 00:02:27.204
Tesla set out on his own hoping to find investors
interested in his concept for an AC motor

00:02:27.204 --> 00:02:28.644
that he had patented.

00:02:28.644 --> 00:02:30.214
Yet he struggled.

00:02:30.214 --> 00:02:33.034
Alternating current was a totally new technology.

00:02:33.034 --> 00:02:34.954
Not something anyone had ever seen before.

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:39.174
He even briefly had to resort to digging ditches
just to survive.

00:02:39.174 --> 00:02:41.874
But there was one person who took a chance
on him.

00:02:41.874 --> 00:02:45.444
Entrepreneur George Westinghouse saw the potential
of AC.

00:02:45.444 --> 00:02:49.664
He believed AC was THE missing link in long-distance
power transmission.

00:02:49.664 --> 00:02:56.014
So he purchased Tesla’s AC patents for $60,000
and offered Tesla stock and royalties of $2.50

00:02:56.014 --> 00:02:58.464
for each horsepower of electricity sold.

00:02:58.464 --> 00:03:00.904
All of this is worth millions today.

00:03:00.904 --> 00:03:05.864
Edison tried to do everything he could to
discredit Tesla’s AC system so that DC would

00:03:05.864 --> 00:03:08.474
be the preferred choice to power homes.

00:03:08.474 --> 00:03:13.104
He found an ally in J.P. Morgan, at the time
the most powerful banker in America.

00:03:13.104 --> 00:03:15.724
This would lead to the birth of General Electric.

00:03:15.724 --> 00:03:19.904
The battle against Tesla and Westinghouse
is now known as the War of the Currents.

00:03:19.904 --> 00:03:24.004
And in many ways, it was a propaganda war
launched by Edison to try to convince the

00:03:24.004 --> 00:03:26.564
public that AC was dangerous.

00:03:26.564 --> 00:03:30.824
He zeroed in on the fact that alternating
current operated at much higher voltages than

00:03:30.824 --> 00:03:32.344
direct current.

00:03:32.344 --> 00:03:36.014
When the state of New York commissioned an
electricity salesman named Harold Brown to

00:03:36.014 --> 00:03:40.684
build the electric chair, Edison paid him
behind the scenes to use his rival’s AC

00:03:40.684 --> 00:03:43.324
generators secured through a second-hand dealer.

00:03:43.324 --> 00:03:47.864
Brown also used AC to electrocute dogs at
Edison’s laboratory in New Jersey, which

00:03:47.864 --> 00:03:52.324
Edison recalled in articles he published,
writing: “I have myself seen a large healthy

00:03:52.324 --> 00:03:55.664
dog killed instantly by the alternating current.”

00:03:55.664 --> 00:03:58.424
Other animals were not immune to his schemes.

00:03:58.424 --> 00:04:04.074
Edison’s company electrocuted a circus elephant
named Topsy using AC and produced a film about

00:04:04.074 --> 00:04:05.074
it.

00:04:05.074 --> 00:04:09.914
His anti-AC crusade may or may not have also
come from a genuine concern about safety.

00:04:09.914 --> 00:04:13.624
He wrote a letter warning the president of
his electric light company of the danger of

00:04:13.624 --> 00:04:18.484
AC, declaring: "Just as certain as death,
Westinghouse will kill a customer within six

00:04:18.484 --> 00:04:21.694
months after he puts in a system of any size."

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:25.894
Sure enough, there were several accidental
deaths due to the high voltages of AC in the

00:04:25.894 --> 00:04:29.384
late 1880s in New York and several other cities.

00:04:29.384 --> 00:04:34.544
In one incident, telegraph lineman John Feeks’
body was left hanging in tangled wires in

00:04:34.544 --> 00:04:36.284
front of a horrified crowd.

00:04:36.284 --> 00:04:40.544
His death led to laws to move AC lines underground
in New York City.

00:04:40.544 --> 00:04:45.584
Tesla felt that the only way to show AC was
safe was to demonstrate it on himself.

00:04:45.584 --> 00:04:50.864
In a dramatic display at Columbia University,
he grasped a brass ball in each hand and touched

00:04:50.864 --> 00:04:56.544
the terminals of a high-voltage, high-frequency
transformer now known as a Tesla coil.

00:04:56.544 --> 00:05:00.394
250,000 volts raced across the surface of
his body.

00:05:00.394 --> 00:05:04.664
A newspaper reported that he was surrounded
by “...tongues of electric flame.”

00:05:04.664 --> 00:05:12.484
In 1893, Tesla and Westinghouse scored a big
win when they beat out Edison to light up

00:05:12.484 --> 00:05:15.674
the Chicago World Fair using alternating current.

00:05:15.674 --> 00:05:18.604
The first all-electric fair in history.

00:05:18.604 --> 00:05:23.134
Three years later, Tesla lit up the city of
Buffalo using AC motors powered by the waters

00:05:23.134 --> 00:05:25.604
of Niagara Falls.

00:05:25.604 --> 00:05:27.854
This was Tesla’s childhood dream.

00:05:27.854 --> 00:05:31.704
As a kid, he saw an image of the Falls and
told his uncle that one day, he’d go to

00:05:31.704 --> 00:05:34.844
America and capture the energy of Niagara.

00:05:34.844 --> 00:05:39.844
The first hydroelectric power plant at Niagara
Falls would mark the beginning of the electrification

00:05:39.844 --> 00:05:41.284
of the world.

00:05:41.284 --> 00:05:45.674
When he gave a speech at the opening ceremony,
Tesla said: “It is a monument worthy of

00:05:45.674 --> 00:05:47.124
our scientific age...”

00:05:47.124 --> 00:05:52.294
“It signifies the subjugation of natural
forces to the service of man, the discontinuance

00:05:52.294 --> 00:05:56.764
of barbarous methods, the relieving of millions
from want and suffering."

00:05:56.764 --> 00:06:03.364
Today, almost every home in the world is powered
by AC whereas DC powers smaller items, anything

00:06:03.364 --> 00:06:07.864
that uses batteries: from your electric toothbrush
to the clock on the wall.

00:06:07.864 --> 00:06:13.354
Despite the success of AC, Tesla never went
on to the fame and fortune that Edison enjoyed.

00:06:13.354 --> 00:06:15.284
Because Edison was not only an inventor.

00:06:15.284 --> 00:06:20.204
He was a businessman, honing his craft at
the age of 13 when he started selling candy,

00:06:20.204 --> 00:06:25.224
newspapers, and vegetables on trains and turned
an amazing profit of $50 a week in his day

00:06:25.224 --> 00:06:27.734
- about $1,600 today.

00:06:27.734 --> 00:06:31.614
Edison would likely have made a different
choice when confronted with the challenging

00:06:31.614 --> 00:06:35.343
situation that Tesla faced next.

00:06:35.343 --> 00:06:40.004
The war of the currents took a financial toll
on Westinghouse whose company racked up millions

00:06:40.004 --> 00:06:41.884
of dollars in debt.

00:06:41.884 --> 00:06:46.314
When Westinghouse begged Tesla to lower his
royalty rate in order to save the company,

00:06:46.314 --> 00:06:48.014
Tesla did more than that.

00:06:48.014 --> 00:06:52.204
He tore up his contract - relinquishing his
royalties altogether.

00:06:52.204 --> 00:06:57.873
It’s estimated that Tesla willingly walked
away from $12 million in royalties in his

00:06:57.873 --> 00:07:01.754
day which today would be worth well over $300
million.

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:06.204
Those royalties were so valuable they would
have accrued over time to turn Tesla into

00:07:06.204 --> 00:07:08.914
the world’s first billionaire.

00:07:08.914 --> 00:07:16.744
In return, Westinghouse paid him a lump sum
of $216,000 for the right to use his AC patents

00:07:16.744 --> 00:07:19.964
forever which is roughly $5 million today.

00:07:19.964 --> 00:07:24.444
Tesla’s decision would hurt him dearly because
he ended up short of cash to fund his other

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:25.854
grand ideas.

00:07:25.854 --> 00:07:30.234
He had discovered that his powerful coil made
it possible to send and receive radio signals.

00:07:30.234 --> 00:07:35.964
But in 1895, a fire broke out at the building
housing his New York lab as he was about to

00:07:35.964 --> 00:07:38.414
transmit a signal 50 miles to West Point,
New York.

00:07:38.414 --> 00:07:43.504
The blaze destroyed everything: his models,
designs, notes, and tools.

00:07:43.504 --> 00:07:48.464
The timing was terrible because Italian inventor
Guglielmo Marconi was also developing radio

00:07:48.464 --> 00:07:51.504
in England, sending Morse-code-based signals.

00:07:51.504 --> 00:07:55.964
Tesla still managed to beat Marconi to the
punch by filing American patents for the radio

00:07:55.964 --> 00:07:58.024
first, in 1897.

00:07:58.024 --> 00:08:02.214
When Marconi tried to do the same a few years
later, the U.S. Patent Office turned him down

00:08:02.214 --> 00:08:04.654
because his were too similar to Tesla’s.

00:08:04.654 --> 00:08:06.593
Repeated applications were rejected.

00:08:06.593 --> 00:08:11.764
But Marconi did attract the attention of none
other than Edison who invested in him and

00:08:11.764 --> 00:08:14.494
became a consulting engineer at Marconi’s
company.

00:08:14.494 --> 00:08:19.974
The Italian’s greatest achievement came
in 1901 when he received the first radio signal

00:08:19.974 --> 00:08:24.794
sent across the Atlantic from a transmitter
in southwest England to St John’s Newfoundland

00:08:24.794 --> 00:08:26.224
in Eastern Canada.

00:08:26.224 --> 00:08:28.814
This won Marconi international fame.

00:08:28.814 --> 00:08:31.714
And he had to use Tesla’s patents to achieve
it.

00:08:31.714 --> 00:08:33.414
But Tesla didn’t mind.

00:08:33.414 --> 00:08:37.024
When one of his engineers said: "Looks as
if Marconi got the jump on you."

00:08:37.024 --> 00:08:39.054
Tesla replied: "Marconi is a good fellow.

00:08:39.054 --> 00:08:40.104
Let him continue.

00:08:40.104 --> 00:08:42.594
He is using seventeen of my patents.”

00:08:42.594 --> 00:08:45.524
Unfortunately, no patent is truly safe.

00:08:45.524 --> 00:08:52.354
In 1904, the Patent Office decided inexplicably
to rewrite history by awarding Marconi a patent

00:08:52.354 --> 00:08:56.194
for the invention of the radio, reversing
its earlier decisions.

00:08:56.194 --> 00:08:58.074
It gave no reason.

00:08:58.074 --> 00:09:02.314
But it didn’t hurt that Marconi had the
financial backing of Edison and other influential

00:09:02.314 --> 00:09:03.314
people.

00:09:03.314 --> 00:09:08.864
In 1909, Marconi won the Nobel Prize in physics
for the invention of the radio.

00:09:08.864 --> 00:09:10.334
Tesla was furious.

00:09:10.334 --> 00:09:13.293
And sued the Marconi Company for infringement.

00:09:13.293 --> 00:09:17.074
The radio incident led Tesla to lose business
opportunities.

00:09:17.074 --> 00:09:20.793
For example, Tesla had a grand vision for
wireless electricity.

00:09:20.793 --> 00:09:26.194
His proposal initially caught the attention
of J.P. Morgan who put up $150,000 to build

00:09:26.194 --> 00:09:29.324
a giant transmission tower in 1901.

00:09:29.324 --> 00:09:35.014
Tesla believed the 187-foot, 57-meter tall
Wardenclyffe Tower in Long Island, New York

00:09:35.014 --> 00:09:39.793
was the start of a system that could deliver
electricity without wires around the globe.

00:09:39.793 --> 00:09:42.964
And anyone with the right equipment could
tap into it.

00:09:42.964 --> 00:09:46.424
He imagined a scenario where “...it will
be possible for a businessman in New York

00:09:46.424 --> 00:09:50.964
to dictate instructions, and have them instantly
appear in type at his office in London or

00:09:50.964 --> 00:09:52.004
elsewhere.”

00:09:52.004 --> 00:09:56.935
It was incredibly prescient to envision telecommunications
infrastructure much like today’s Internet

00:09:56.935 --> 00:09:58.334
more than a century ago.

00:09:58.334 --> 00:10:01.603
But Tesla’s vision would never bear fruit.

00:10:01.603 --> 00:10:05.004
Electricity can indeed be transmitted through
the air but it’s impractical because of

00:10:05.004 --> 00:10:07.264
the amount of power needed to make it happen.

00:10:07.264 --> 00:10:10.114
So, Tesla appealed to Morgan for more money.

00:10:10.114 --> 00:10:11.794
It was all for naught.

00:10:11.794 --> 00:10:15.944
Morgan was throwing money in the other direction
toward Marconi.

00:10:15.944 --> 00:10:20.344
Wardenclyffe Tower was never completed and
would eventually be demolished, along with

00:10:20.344 --> 00:10:21.974
Tesla’s vision.

00:10:21.974 --> 00:10:26.824
Tesla was devastated and had a breakdown,
lamenting: ”It is not a dream.

00:10:26.824 --> 00:10:32.983
It is a simple feat of scientific electrical
engineering, only expensive—blind, faint-hearted,

00:10:32.983 --> 00:10:34.774
doubting world!”

00:10:34.774 --> 00:10:37.464
Tesla began to withdraw from that world.

00:10:37.464 --> 00:10:42.404
He showed signs of obsessive-compulsive disorder
which compelled him to do things in threes,

00:10:42.404 --> 00:10:46.483
including living in a hotel room that was
divisible by the number three.

00:10:46.483 --> 00:10:50.354
He spent the last decade of his life living
out of a hotel paid for by the Westinghouse

00:10:50.354 --> 00:10:53.154
Corporation which kept him on as a consultant.

00:10:53.154 --> 00:10:54.684
He was broke.

00:10:54.684 --> 00:10:56.414
And befriended pigeons.

00:10:56.414 --> 00:11:00.194
Edison on the other hand continued to reap
immense rewards.

00:11:00.194 --> 00:11:02.804
Businesses and partnerships filled his life.

00:11:02.804 --> 00:11:08.043
By the time he died in 1931, he patented a
record 1,093 inventions.

00:11:08.043 --> 00:11:12.914
There was the lightbulb, of course, but also
the motion picture camera, and the early record

00:11:12.914 --> 00:11:14.764
player called a phonograph.

00:11:14.764 --> 00:11:18.674
Today - he is considered one of the world’s
greatest inventors.

00:11:18.674 --> 00:11:24.293
It was only after Tesla’s death that he
got a modicum of justice for all that he had

00:11:24.293 --> 00:11:25.334
lost.

00:11:25.334 --> 00:11:29.444
When Tesla sued Marconi, the case dragged
on in court for years.

00:11:29.444 --> 00:11:34.434
Six months after his passing in 1943, the
U.S. Supreme Court ruled Marconi’s radio

00:11:34.434 --> 00:11:38.994
patents were invalid and awarded the patents
for the radio to Tesla.

00:11:38.994 --> 00:11:41.414
But it had a selfish reason for doing so.

00:11:41.414 --> 00:11:45.784
Marconi’s company had sued the U.S. government
for patent infringement during World War I.

00:11:45.784 --> 00:11:51.324
So to avoid the lawsuit altogether, the Supreme
Court deemed Tesla the inventor of the radio.

00:11:51.324 --> 00:11:56.674
Today, at the site where Tesla’s Wardenclyffe
Tower once stood, is a science museum dedicated

00:11:56.674 --> 00:11:59.754
to the man whose dreams were not to be.

00:11:59.754 --> 00:12:03.704
The Tesla Science Center was made possible
thanks to the financial support of thousands

00:12:03.704 --> 00:12:07.824
of Tesla fans, including Tesla CEO Elon Musk.

00:12:07.824 --> 00:12:12.944
Tesla’s prediction of transmitting data
without wires came true in the early nineties.

00:12:12.944 --> 00:12:17.274
The internet has changed our lives yet at
the same time, it’s important to stay protected

00:12:17.274 --> 00:12:18.274
